From: AW1000_NSS_BYTE
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: declare the two non-static nss_core.c entry points

Kernel 6.18 enables -Wmissing-prototypes for every object it compiles, and this
tree builds with -Werror, so a non-static function with no prototype is a hard
error:

    nss_core.c:392:6: error: no previous prototype for
        'nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt' [-Werror=missing-prototypes]
    nss_core.c:2762:6: error: no previous prototype for 'nss_skb_reuse'
    cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

Both are internal to the driver, but nss_skb_reuse() is also referenced from
nss_init.c's sysctl table, so neither can simply be made static without moving
code across translation units.

Passing -Wno-missing-prototypes through ccflags-y does not work:
ccflags-y given on the make command line is a command-line variable, which
overrides every `ccflags-y += ...` in the driver's own Makefile. The build then
loses all of its -DNSS_DRV_*_ENABLE feature defines.

Declare the two functions in nss_core.h, anchored on
nss_core_init_handlers() rather than a line number: the nss feed's own patches
insert several hundred lines above that point, so a hunk written against
pristine upstream (where it was line 951) does not reach it here.

The context is kept deliberately short. GNU patch 2.8.1, which the build image
ships, fails to match a hunk of this size when the surrounding context lines
are the long prototypes in this header; a three-line hunk matches cleanly.

--- a/nss_core.h
+++ b/nss_core.h
@@ -997,3 +997,5 @@
 extern void nss_core_init_handlers(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx);
+extern void nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx, struct sk_buff *nbuf);
+extern void nss_skb_reuse(struct sk_buff *nbuf);
 void nss_core_update_max_ipv4_conn(int conn);
 void nss_core_update_max_ipv6_conn(int conn);
